llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232, applied after
140): the MoE expert prefetch (patches 108 and 126) decides once which split
inputs live in its slots, and zeroes the padding after each slot copy's last
row after every copy. Not gfx803-specific code; found and measured on gfx803.

WHY
---
Without this patch, prompt processing of a MoE model whose experts stay in
system RAM gives wrong logits when a layer reads a view of a host weight, and
NaN when an expert matrix's rows are not a whole number of MMQ K blocks.
Gemma 4 26B-A4B does both; Qwen3.6 35B-A3B does neither and is unaffected.

1. Views of host weights. The prefetch places a split's host weights in a
   slot when it assigns the slots, and at compute time the scheduler skips
   the normal copy of every input that ggml_backend_sched_is_host_weight()
   accepts, because the prefetch copies those. That test reads the tensor's
   buffer. A view gets its buffer only when the graph allocator initializes
   views, which happens after the slots are assigned. Gemma 4 scales each
   expert's down projection by a reshaped view of blk.N.ffn_down_exps.scale,
   an input of every down-projection split. At assignment the view had no
   buffer, so it got no slot and the graph allocator placed its copy in the
   compute buffer. At compute time it had one, so the normal copy was skipped
   and the prefetch copied it on the copy stream into that compute-buffer
   address. That copy is ordered only against the slot events, not against
   the splits that reuse the compute buffer, so its 512 bytes landed in live
   activations of the splits running at that moment. The damage depends on
   timing: it changes with the number of slots and with SDMA on or off, and
   it disappears when the GPU is serialized. It is not a copy engine or HIP
   runtime fault: plain HIP reproductions of the slot schedule (pinned and
   pageable sources, 4 KiB to 520 MiB slots, copies overlapping
   memory-heavy kernels) are correct with the image's HIP runtime and with
   AMD's stock one, and the failure occurs with both.

2. Padding. The CUDA/HIP buffer type pads a quantized tensor after its last
   row (MATRIX_ROW_PADDING), and MMQ reads that padding when a row is not a
   whole number of its K blocks: Gemma 4's down projections are 704 wide
   (Q5_0 and Q8_0 in alternate layers), Qwen3.8-Flash's 640. Patch 108 zeroes
   a slot only when it allocates it. The slots are then reused by splits
   with other layouts, so a tensor's padding holds bytes of whatever tensor
   the previous user placed there; read as Q5_0 blocks, some of them decode
   to inf or NaN scales, and inf or NaN times the zero activations past the
   row is NaN. A probe of the shipped scheduler found 12 of 60 such paddings
   nonzero during a 385-token Gemma 4 batch.

Measurements, RX 590, Gemma 4 26B-A4B-it heretic-ara i1-Q4_K_M, experts in
system RAM, llama-perplexity KL divergence against the CPU backend over 8
chunks of 512 tokens of fiction prose (-ub 512, q8_0 KV cache, flash
attention). Gemma 4 is very sensitive to rounding: two CPU code paths (with
and without weight repacking) already differ by 0.36.

| | mean KLD | same top token |
| --- | --- | --- |
| CPU without repack | 0.36 | 82.0% |
| stock llama.cpp b12340 on the GPU | 0.43 | 80.9% |
| before, weights memory-mapped | 4.46 | 35.2% |
| before, weights pinned (--load-mode none) | 9.76 | 9.3% |
| before, GGML_SCHED_EXPERT_PREFETCH=0 | 0.41 | 81.4% |
| this patch, pinned or memory-mapped | 0.41 | 81.4% |

With this patch the logits are byte-identical to the run without prefetch.

WHAT
----
- ggml_backend_sched_assign_expert_slots records in expert_in_slot which
  inputs of which split it placed in a slot. ggml_backend_sched_prefetch_split
  copies exactly those, and the compute loop skips exactly those; every other
  input, the view included, takes the normal copy path as without prefetch.
- For each slot copy whose allocation is larger than the tensor, the
  assignment places an I8 tensor over the padding, and the prefetch zeroes it
  from a host buffer of zeros on the copy stream right after the tensor's
  copy. The zeros buffer only grows, after a synchronization, because a
  queued copy may still be reading it.

VERIFIED ON REAL HARDWARE (RX 590), in the image built with this patch:
- Gemma 4 26B-A4B as above, prefetch on, pinned weights: mean KLD 0.413499,
  the same value as with GGML_SCHED_EXPERT_PREFETCH=0.
- Qwen3.6 35B-A3B heretic Q4_K_M with its hot list, through llama-server:
  next-token distributions after 20-1,200 words of prose agree with the CPU
  backend to a mean KL divergence of 0.046 with and without this patch;
  eight ~2,000-token fiction prompts, 400 greedy tokens each: prompts 545 /
  548 tok/s, generation 39.75 / 39.53 tok/s without / with it. No GPU fault.
